# The product's own TypeScript program

TypeScript 6 is a JavaScript library, so the editor can run it inside its own process. `createProductProgram` builds a program on it for a project that installs TypeScript 6: a standalone TypeScript service, governed by the consumer's `tsconfig.json`, so each section is analyzed the way the project ships it. The program runs on the TypeScript 6 the host loads for the project when the project first asks, and on nothing else.

The program never opens a `.loom`. The host composes the file's whole corpus, every composition root resolved to TypeScript, and hands the program each root under its own path. One root's `import` of another then resolves against that other's composition, not its last output on disk. The program holds every root, but it names to TypeScript only the roots of its own project, so TypeScript parses those roots and what they import rather than the whole corpus. Volar's `volar-service-typescript` plugins answer diagnostics, hover, completion, and definition over this program. The host maps each answer back to its place in the `.loom`.

## What the program offers

A `ProductProgram` holds the product's roots and answers the editor's questions about them. `sync` sets those roots in one call. It takes the roots the program lists, each a `ProductFile` with its path and text, and the other roots of the composition, which the program reads only when a listed root imports one. A program called with no other roots lists every root it holds. A `ProductFile` may also name the `.loom` it was composed from, and a root marked `loose` is a section that tangles to no file, composed under a name of its own beside its loom. The TypeScript 6 program needs neither and treats every root alike; the native program uses both to decide which roots it serves. `sync` adds the roots new since the last call, replaces the ones whose text changed, and drops the ones now gone. The feature methods answer over a root by its path: `diagnostics` for the whole file; `hover`, `completions`, `definition`, and `references` at a position; `rename` for a new name; and `codeActions` for the quick-fixes and refactors in a range, with `resolveCodeAction` filling in an action's edit, given the root the action was listed for. The TypeScript 6 program finds that root in the action's own data and ignores the path, while the native program asks its server about that root. Three more ask only about the code's shape: `documentSymbols` for the outline of a root's symbols, `foldingRanges` for the ranges an editor folds, and `selectionRanges` for the ranges a selection grows through from each position. Each returns what `volar-service-typescript` finds, named by that path. The service rewrites each such path to the embedded document it stands for, and Volar maps the coordinates back to the `.loom`. `dispose` releases the service.

=>

export interface ProductFile {
  readonly path: string
  readonly text: string
  readonly loose?: boolean
  readonly loom?: string
}

export interface ProductProgram {
  readonly sync: (
    listed: ReadonlyArray<ProductFile>,
    others?: ReadonlyArray<ProductFile>,
  ) => void
  readonly diagnostics: (fileName: string) => Promise<Diagnostic[]>
  readonly hover: (fileName: string, position: Position) => Promise<Hover | undefined>
  readonly completions: (fileName: string, position: Position) => Promise<CompletionList>
  readonly definition: (fileName: string, position: Position) => Promise<LocationLink[] | undefined>
  readonly references: (fileName: string, position: Position) => Promise<Location[] | undefined>
  readonly rename: (
    fileName: string,
    position: Position,
    newName: string,
  ) => Promise<WorkspaceEdit | undefined>
  readonly codeActions: (
    fileName: string,
    range: Range,
    context: CodeActionContext,
  ) => Promise<CodeAction[] | undefined>
  readonly resolveCodeAction: (action: CodeAction, fileName: string) => Promise<CodeAction>
  readonly documentSymbols: (fileName: string) => Promise<DocumentSymbol[] | undefined>
  readonly foldingRanges: (fileName: string) => Promise<FoldingRange[] | undefined>
  readonly selectionRanges: (
    fileName: string,
    positions: Position[],
  ) => Promise<SelectionRange[] | undefined>
  readonly dispose: () => void
}

## The files served from memory

Volar reads files through a language: a registry that holds a snapshot of each file, the file's text as Volar last read it. `productLanguage` builds one that serves the product roots from memory and every other file from disk. It answers a root by file name, from the `roots` map the program owns. It reads every other file Volar asks for, such as the TypeScript standard library or a package under `node_modules`, from disk. A file that is neither a root nor present on disk leaves the registry.

=>

export const productLanguage = (
  ts: TypeScript,
  roots: ReadonlyMap<string, IScriptSnapshot>,
): Language<URI> => {
  const fromDisk = diskSnapshots(ts)
  const language = createLanguage<URI>(
    [{ getLanguageId: (uri: URI) => resolveFileLanguageId(uri.path) }],
    createUriMap(ts.sys.useCaseSensitiveFileNames),
    (uri, includeFsFiles) => {
      const fileName = uri.fsPath
      const root = roots.get(fileName)
      if (root) {
        language.scripts.set(uri, root)
        return
      }
      if (!includeFsFiles) return
      Option.match(fromDisk(fileName), {
        onSome: (snapshot) => language.scripts.set(uri, snapshot),
        onNone: () => language.scripts.delete(uri),
      })
    },
  )
  return language
}

~

A file read from disk keeps its snapshot until the file changes on disk. Volar versions a file by its snapshot, so every new snapshot is a new version, even one that holds the same text. TypeScript parses a file again whenever its version moves. Volar looks a file up each time TypeScript asks for the file's version, so a file read afresh on every lookup would never keep a version. Each edit to a root would then rebuild the program from nothing. TypeScript would parse the standard library and every declaration file under `node_modules` again. That takes seconds, and every other request from the editor waits behind it, a format among them.

`diskSnapshots` builds the reader that `productLanguage` calls `fromDisk`. The reader reads each file through `ts.sys`, the TypeScript system interface, and keeps it in a `DiskFile`, which records the file's snapshot and the time the file was last modified. `fromDisk` reads a file again only when that time moves. A file it found missing is kept too, so the next lookup asks the disk only for the time. Volar's own language server keeps the files it reads the same way.

=>

interface DiskFile {
  readonly modified: Option.Option<number>
  readonly snapshot: Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot>
}

const sameTime = Option.makeEquivalence(Equivalence.Number)

const diskSnapshots = (ts: TypeScript): ((fileName: string) => Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot>) => {
  const disk = new Map<string, DiskFile>()
  const read = (fileName: string, modified: Option.Option<number>): Option.Option<IScriptSnapshot> => {
    const snapshot = Option.map(
      Option.fromNullishOr(ts.sys.fileExists(fileName) ? ts.sys.readFile(fileName) : undefined),
      (text) => ts.ScriptSnapshot.fromString(text),
    )
    disk.set(fileName, { modified, snapshot })
    return snapshot
  }
  return (fileName) => {
    const modified = Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(ts.sys.getModifiedTime?.(fileName)), (time) =>
      time.valueOf(),
    )
    return pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(disk.get(fileName)),
      Option.filter((file) => sameTime(file.modified, modified)),
      Option.match({
        onSome: (file) => file.snapshot,
        onNone: () => read(fileName, modified),
      }),
    )
  }
}

## Assembling the program

`createProductProgram` ties the layers together. It owns the `roots` map of every root it holds, the `fileNames` set of the roots it names to TypeScript, and a project version, and `sync` keeps all three current. It drops the roots the new set omits, writes the ones whose text changed, takes the new listing, and bumps the version once when any of them changed. The program then rechecks after a real edit and rests otherwise. `unchanged` is the guard `sync` uses, comparing a root's incoming text against the snapshot already held. The project host reports the compiler options, that version, and the listed roots as the program's file names. `createLanguageServiceHost` turns it into the host TypeScript needs. `createLanguageService` runs the feature plugins over that host. The returned `ProductProgram` forwards each feature to it, naming the file with `URI.file` and passing the position through unchanged.

=>

export const createProductProgram = (
  ts: TypeScript,
  compilerOptions: CompilerOptions,
): ProductProgram => {
  const roots = new Map<string, IScriptSnapshot>()
  let fileNames: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set()
  let projectVersion = 0
  const language = productLanguage(ts, roots)
  const asUri = (fileName: string): URI => URI.file(fileName)

  const projectHost = {
    getCurrentDirectory: () => process.cwd(),
    getCompilationSettings: () => compilerOptions,
    getProjectVersion: () => projectVersion.toString(),
    getScriptFileNames: () => [...fileNames],
  }

  const env: LanguageServiceEnvironment = {
    workspaceFolders: [URI.file(process.cwd())],
  }

  const service = createLanguageService(language, createTypescriptServices(ts), env, {
    typescript: {
      configFileName: undefined,
      sys: ts.sys,
      uriConverter: { asFileName: (uri: URI) => uri.fsPath, asUri },
      ...createLanguageServiceHost(ts, ts.sys, language, asUri, projectHost),
    },
  })

  const unchanged = (path: string, text: string): boolean =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(roots.get(path)),
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => false,
        onSome: (snapshot) => snapshot.getText(0, snapshot.getLength()) === text,
      }),
    )

  const sync = (
    listed: ReadonlyArray<ProductFile>,
    others: ReadonlyArray<ProductFile> = [],
  ): void => {
    const files = [...listed, ...others]
    const present = new Set(Array.map(files, (file) => file.path))
    const stale = Array.filter(Array.fromIterable(roots.keys()), (path) => !present.has(path))
    const fresh = Array.filter(files, (file) => !unchanged(file.path, file.text))
    const listing = new Set(Array.map(listed, (file) => file.path))
    const relisted =
      listing.size !== fileNames.size ||
      Array.some(Array.fromIterable(listing), (path) => !fileNames.has(path))
    if (stale.length === 0 && fresh.length === 0 && !relisted) return
    stale.forEach((path) => {
      roots.delete(path)
      language.scripts.delete(asUri(path))
    })
    fresh.forEach((file) => {
      const snapshot = ts.ScriptSnapshot.fromString(file.text)
      roots.set(file.path, snapshot)
      language.scripts.set(asUri(file.path), snapshot)
    })
    fileNames = listing
    projectVersion++
  }

  return {
    sync,
    diagnostics: (fileName) => service.getDiagnostics(asUri(fileName)),
    hover: (fileName, position) => service.getHover(asUri(fileName), position),
    completions: (fileName, position) => service.getCompletionItems(asUri(fileName), position),
    definition: (fileName, position) => service.getDefinition(asUri(fileName), position),
    references: (fileName, position) =>
      service.getReferences(asUri(fileName), position, {
        includeDeclaration: true,
      }),
    rename: (fileName, position, newName) =>
      service.getRenameEdits(asUri(fileName), position, newName),
    codeActions: (fileName, range, context) =>
      service.getCodeActions(asUri(fileName), range, context),
    resolveCodeAction: (action) => service.resolveCodeAction(action),
    documentSymbols: (fileName) => service.getDocumentSymbols(asUri(fileName)),
    foldingRanges: (fileName) => service.getFoldingRanges(asUri(fileName)),
    selectionRanges: (fileName, positions) =>
      service.getSelectionRanges(asUri(fileName), positions),
    dispose: () => service.dispose(),
  }
}

